00:23:32.932 --> 00:23:34.962
So next I'd like to briefly

00:23:34.962 --> 00:23:37.201
introduce our vision of the Lifeline

00:23:37.202 --> 00:23:39.452
agent and some of our earlier work

00:23:39.612 --> 00:23:41.242
that devoted in this area.

00:23:41.622 --> 00:23:43.702
We view a Lifelong agent not only

00:23:43.702 --> 00:23:45.692
as a system that is trained once and

00:23:45.932 --> 00:23:48.011
then deployed once and fixed, but as

00:23:48.011 --> 00:23:49.631
one continuously learn

00:23:50.232 --> 00:23:52.491
align, and evolve over time, As shown

00:23:52.492 --> 00:23:54.812
in the figure, so you can see the agent

00:23:54.812 --> 00:23:56.822
interacts with the user and

00:23:56.882 --> 00:23:59.072
the environment. And improves through

00:23:59.072 --> 00:24:01.241
feedback and experience. And

00:24:01.242 --> 00:24:03.401
gradually updates his internal decision

00:24:03.401 --> 00:24:05.432
making and becomes more

00:24:05.432 --> 00:24:07.212
capable and reliable over time.

00:24:07.652 --> 00:24:09.901
At the same time, this process must remain

00:24:10.121 --> 00:24:11.742
grounded in human preferences.

00:24:12.261 --> 00:24:13.482
Real world constraints,

00:24:14.402 --> 00:24:15.782
efficiency, and the sustainability.

00:24:16.642 --> 00:24:18.802
So the VisionConnect, so several directions

00:24:18.802 --> 00:24:21.122
we have been working on, including the agent

00:24:21.122 --> 00:24:22.742
post training, preference alignment,

00:24:23.262 --> 00:24:25.342
self evolution, real world agents, and also

00:24:25.342 --> 00:24:27.482
multi agent systems. So

00:24:27.482 --> 00:24:29.562
through this workshop, we hope to bring all

00:24:29.562 --> 00:24:31.892
these threads together and move towards agent

00:24:31.972 --> 00:24:34.362
that can adapt to responsibly and robustly

00:24:34.362 --> 00:24:35.752
through the life span.

00:24:38.222 --> 00:24:40.192
So the foundation

00:24:40.252 --> 00:24:42.272
of the life line age actually begins with

00:24:42.412 --> 00:24:44.462
the self awareness and the environment

00:24:45.022 --> 00:24:47.182
mental comprehension. So before an agent

00:24:47.182 --> 00:24:49.312
can evolve or interact

00:24:49.312 --> 00:24:51.652
safely, it must first understand

00:24:51.652 --> 00:24:53.811
the boundary of his own internal knowledge

00:24:54.051 --> 00:24:56.211
and possess a functioning predictive model

00:24:56.212 --> 00:24:58.151
of the world it operates within.

00:24:58.572 --> 00:25:01.072
So this phase establishes the baseline

00:25:01.132 --> 00:25:03.452
decision making logic that governs

00:25:03.452 --> 00:25:05.232
all subsequent agent behaviors.

00:25:07.732 --> 00:25:09.852
So let's start by

00:25:09.852 --> 00:25:12.252
looking at the very foundation of a Lifelong

00:25:12.252 --> 00:25:14.722
agent. So before an agent can

00:25:14.862 --> 00:25:17.152
actually learn or evolve it has to answer

00:25:17.152 --> 00:25:19.252
one simple question first. So

00:25:19.612 --> 00:25:21.807
when should I actually take an

00:25:21.807 --> 00:25:23.952
action in the outside world? So

00:25:23.952 --> 00:25:26.022
right now, many AI models they

00:25:26.022 --> 00:25:27.642
just use the tools by default,

00:25:28.462 --> 00:25:30.542
and they search the web or call

00:25:30.542 --> 00:25:32.871
APIs even when they don't need to.

00:25:32.872 --> 00:25:35.031
So which was a lot of time and also

00:25:35.032 --> 00:25:37.202
computing resource, as you can

00:25:37.202 --> 00:25:39.362
see in this diagram, we show on

00:25:39.362 --> 00:25:41.421
the slides, so we view the agent

00:25:41.421 --> 00:25:43.762
as a goal oriented decision maker.

00:25:44.582 --> 00:25:46.602
Standing between two worlds. So on the

00:25:46.602 --> 00:25:49.022
top, it has the internal cognitive tools.

00:25:49.492 --> 00:25:51.592
This is the agent thinking step by step

00:25:52.122 --> 00:25:54.222
reflecting and using its own memory.

00:25:54.702 --> 00:25:56.942
On the bottom, it has external physical

00:25:56.942 --> 00:25:59.171
tool, like doing a web search or

00:25:59.172 --> 00:26:01.002
running a calculator or something like that.

00:26:01.482 --> 00:26:03.642
So our theory of agent introduces a

00:26:03.642 --> 00:26:05.222
simple rule to manage this.

00:26:05.982 --> 00:26:08.222
Agent should only cross that two use

00:26:08.222 --> 00:26:10.491
decision boundary in the middle when it

00:26:10.492 --> 00:26:12.822
absolutely has to. So we

00:26:12.822 --> 00:26:14.782
call this epidemic necessity.

00:26:15.102 --> 00:26:17.262
So basically, the agent should first

00:26:17.262 --> 00:26:19.282
try to solve the problem using what

00:26:19.782 --> 00:26:22.202
it already knows inside its head,

00:26:22.492 --> 00:26:24.911
only when it hits a wall and it genuinely lacks

00:26:25.212 --> 00:26:27.342
the information. That's also when it

00:26:27.342 --> 00:26:29.742
reaches the knowledge boundary

00:26:29.742 --> 00:26:31.762
should it reach out and use an external

00:26:31.901 --> 00:26:33.902
tool. So by perfectly aligning

00:26:33.902 --> 00:26:35.922
the decision to act with the limits

00:26:36.082 --> 00:26:37.862
of what the agent actually knows,

00:26:38.162 --> 00:26:40.242
we stop the agent from overthinking or

00:26:40.242 --> 00:26:42.462
overacting And this basic

00:26:42.462 --> 00:26:44.882
self awareness makes the agent more efficient

00:26:45.092 --> 00:26:47.231
And it is the absolute foundation

00:26:47.232 --> 00:26:49.012
of a system that can operate

00:26:49.882 --> 00:26:51.361
sustainably over a lifelong period.

00:26:54.682 --> 00:26:56.612
So moving on to our next point.

00:26:56.772 --> 00:26:59.012
Once the agent decides it needs to take

00:26:59.012 --> 00:27:01.312
an action, it should really know what

00:27:01.312 --> 00:27:03.482
might happen before it actually does.

00:27:03.801 --> 00:27:05.881
So if you look at the top left of

00:27:05.882 --> 00:27:08.012
this figure, part a, you will see

00:27:08.762 --> 00:27:11.081
this paper search approach. So instead

00:27:11.082 --> 00:27:13.242
of learning by trial and

00:27:13.242 --> 00:27:15.562
error in the real world, which can be slow,

00:27:15.562 --> 00:27:17.622
costly, and risky, we use

00:27:17.622 --> 00:27:19.722
the language model itself as a simulated

00:27:20.182 --> 00:27:22.282
sandbox. So we call this text

00:27:22.282 --> 00:27:24.342
based world model. But how

00:27:24.342 --> 00:27:26.042
do we know if the submission

00:27:26.602 --> 00:27:28.792
this simulation is any good

00:27:29.352 --> 00:27:31.512
so part b outlines our three step

00:27:31.512 --> 00:27:33.622
check So first, simulation needs to

00:27:33.622 --> 00:27:35.941
be highly accurate and consistent with

00:27:35.942 --> 00:27:37.852
reality. And second,

00:27:38.151 --> 00:27:40.522
we feed the model more data. It's

00:27:41.002 --> 00:27:43.242
ability to predict the future state to scale

00:27:43.242 --> 00:27:45.371
us up predictably and gets

00:27:45.372 --> 00:27:47.391
even better. And finally, having

00:27:47.392 --> 00:27:49.512
the internal sandbox gives a lifelong

00:27:49.512 --> 00:27:51.052
agent three huge advantages.

00:27:51.911 --> 00:27:53.942
First, it can verify action to

00:27:53.942 --> 00:27:56.061
make sure it is safe. Before

00:27:56.222 --> 00:27:58.301
before pulling the triggers. And second,

00:27:58.301 --> 00:28:00.322
it can generate a synthetic trajectories

00:28:00.832 --> 00:28:03.152
which basically means it creates its

00:28:03.152 --> 00:28:05.161
own scenarios to practice on.

00:28:05.162 --> 00:28:07.402
And third, it provides a warm start for

00:28:07.402 --> 00:28:09.782
the reinforcement learning giving the agent

00:28:10.002 --> 00:28:12.322
a massive head start so it learns much faster

00:28:12.322 --> 00:28:14.502
when it finally enters the

00:28:14.502 --> 00:28:16.932
real world. So this ability to safely

00:28:16.992 --> 00:28:19.122
practice and predict a game

00:28:19.122 --> 00:28:21.032
changer for the continuous learning.

00:28:22.792 --> 00:28:24.962
So So now, based on what we

00:28:24.962 --> 00:28:26.992
just discussed through my think that the problem

00:28:26.992 --> 00:28:29.232
is already solved. Right? So we just give

00:28:29.232 --> 00:28:31.282
the agent a world model, and it

00:28:31.282 --> 00:28:33.362
will perfectly simulate everything before it

00:28:33.362 --> 00:28:35.302
acts. But our very recent

00:28:35.442 --> 00:28:37.561
research introduces a crucial reality check

00:28:37.801 --> 00:28:39.961
So in this paper, we asked a very simple and

00:28:39.962 --> 00:28:42.222
practical question. Do current

00:28:42.762 --> 00:28:44.702
agents actually use these sandbox

00:28:45.182 --> 00:28:47.422
effectively? And surprisingly, the answer is no.

00:28:47.761 --> 00:28:50.102
We discovered a few surprising things

00:28:50.582 --> 00:28:52.822
So first, models rarely choose to invoke the world

00:28:52.822 --> 00:28:54.852
model on their own. They

00:28:54.852 --> 00:28:57.141
prefer to trust a guess or act blindly.

00:28:57.302 --> 00:28:59.312
And second, even when they

00:28:59.312 --> 00:29:01.322
do use the world model, it

00:29:01.322 --> 00:29:03.502
doesn't reliably improve their

00:29:03.562 --> 00:29:05.882
performance So the net impact is basically

00:29:05.942 --> 00:29:08.222
neutral. So this is a massive

00:29:08.281 --> 00:29:10.491
bottleneck It shows us that simply giving

00:29:10.492 --> 00:29:12.732
an agent a world model isn't actually

00:29:12.732 --> 00:29:14.911
enough. For a lifelong agent

00:29:14.912 --> 00:29:16.852
to actually benefit from foresight,

00:29:17.882 --> 00:29:20.122
we have to build mechanisms that teach it how

00:29:20.122 --> 00:29:21.822
to reliably interact

00:29:22.242 --> 00:29:24.272
with these simulations. So

00:29:24.272 --> 00:29:26.532
it needs to know when to trust the simulation,

00:29:27.272 --> 00:29:29.212
and when to ignore it. And that realization

00:29:30.072 --> 00:29:32.532
naturally leads us to our next major

00:29:32.752 --> 00:29:34.982
topic. So how to teach agent

00:29:34.982 --> 00:29:36.502
self awareness meta cognitions.

00:29:38.382 --> 00:29:40.461
So with the epidemic boundaries

00:29:40.462 --> 00:29:42.222
and predictive world models, it's

00:29:42.622 --> 00:29:44.562
established, the lifeline agent must be aggressively

00:29:44.862 --> 00:29:47.132
optimized for deployment in the

00:29:47.132 --> 00:29:49.361
world. And real world constraints

00:29:49.362 --> 00:29:51.432
demands that the agent

00:29:51.432 --> 00:29:53.712
operates with maximal efficiency

00:29:54.682 --> 00:29:56.982
actively mitigating the computational cost

00:29:57.862 --> 00:30:00.182
and the latency associated with unnecessary tool

00:30:00.182 --> 00:30:00.542
cost.

00:30:02.542 --> 00:30:04.962
So now let's look at a very practical

00:30:05.102 --> 00:30:06.482
challenge for agents.

00:30:07.192 --> 00:30:09.302
To operate in the real world. So

00:30:09.302 --> 00:30:11.162
this challenge is called tool overuse.

00:30:11.572 --> 00:30:13.581
Just because an agent can use

00:30:13.982 --> 00:30:16.222
search engine or calculator, it doesn't mean it

00:30:16.222 --> 00:30:18.242
always should. So if you look

00:30:18.242 --> 00:30:20.281
at the right image, so we solve

00:30:20.281 --> 00:30:22.222
this by teaching the agent meta cognition,

00:30:22.442 --> 00:30:24.322
which is basically the ability

00:30:24.462 --> 00:30:26.242
to think about its own reasoning.

00:30:27.272 --> 00:30:29.432
So let's say you ask the agent

