00:49:11.232 --> 00:49:13.252
We But, now let's talk about the technical

00:49:13.252 --> 00:49:15.631
aspects of this. Okay. So robotic

00:49:15.632 --> 00:49:18.002
foundation models, at the end of the day, not that

00:49:18.002 --> 00:49:20.242
different from regular foundation models. You're

00:49:20.242 --> 00:49:22.362
training a language model, a video generation model, what have

00:49:22.362 --> 00:49:24.631
you, typically the recipe follows this kind of

00:49:24.632 --> 00:49:26.781
structure. You have a pretraining phase

00:49:26.782 --> 00:49:28.942
where you use large amounts of data

00:49:28.942 --> 00:49:31.072
sourced from the web, to pretrain

00:49:31.072 --> 00:49:33.152
your model. And the purpose of the pretraining phase is to

00:49:33.152 --> 00:49:35.462
gain lots of It's not necessarily telling

00:49:35.762 --> 00:49:37.772
the model what it should do in response to queries,

00:49:37.932 --> 00:49:40.012
but it's baking lots of knowledge about the world into

00:49:40.012 --> 00:49:42.252
it. And then you have a post training phase where

00:49:42.252 --> 00:49:44.652
the goal is really to take that knowledge and align

00:49:44.652 --> 00:49:46.702
it with the objectives of the model to

00:49:46.702 --> 00:49:48.802
explain to it how it should follow instructions

00:49:49.242 --> 00:49:50.702
satisfy user preferences.

00:49:51.382 --> 00:49:53.472
So, in robotics, we have basically the same thing.

00:49:53.632 --> 00:49:55.792
There's a pretraining phase where we would like

00:49:55.792 --> 00:49:58.262
to use as much data as possible, the most diverse

00:49:58.422 --> 00:50:00.412
not necessarily the highest quality data,

00:50:00.571 --> 00:50:02.651
data that illustrates many different kinds of

00:50:02.652 --> 00:50:04.712
physical interactions. And then we have

00:50:04.712 --> 00:50:06.812
a post training phase and this is where

00:50:07.132 --> 00:50:09.372
there's room for a lot of really exciting research

00:50:09.452 --> 00:50:11.851
we can do many similar things as in an LLM

00:50:11.851 --> 00:50:14.161
world in terms of instruction fine tuning and so on.

00:50:14.321 --> 00:50:16.382
But we can also do RL. And

00:50:16.522 --> 00:50:18.652
RL has, perhaps

00:50:18.652 --> 00:50:20.812
even more importance in the world of robotics

00:50:20.812 --> 00:50:23.132
than it does in the world of LMs because

00:50:23.132 --> 00:50:25.072
for robots, you have to get very

00:50:25.551 --> 00:50:27.872
nuanced, sophisticated physical behaviors

00:50:27.872 --> 00:50:30.142
that are robust and that succeed reliably

00:50:30.282 --> 00:50:32.161
at complex tasks, which is

00:50:32.402 --> 00:50:34.422
difficult to do purely from large

00:50:34.482 --> 00:50:36.812
scale human generated retraining data.

00:50:37.132 --> 00:50:39.371
So it's really this phase of the process that I'm gonna

00:50:39.372 --> 00:50:41.452
focus on today and I'll describe some

00:50:41.452 --> 00:50:43.152
unique challenges in RL

00:50:44.322 --> 00:50:46.642
training of robotic foundation models, and then provide

00:50:46.642 --> 00:50:48.941
