really tweak the algorithm to be very good at

00:54:05.572 --> 00:54:07.532
very quickly learning a very specific task

00:54:07.852 --> 00:54:10.092
The task you're seeing here is, attaching

00:54:10.092 --> 00:54:12.331
a chip to a PCB board. It finishes training

00:54:12.331 --> 00:54:14.412
in about fifteen minutes and performs the task at

00:54:14.412 --> 00:54:15.762
100% success rate.

00:54:16.802 --> 00:54:18.742
So the algorithm's RLP. It's an off policy actor

00:54:19.362 --> 00:54:20.592
critic algorithm initialized from

00:54:24.812 --> 00:54:27.002
So observations are images

00:54:27.002 --> 00:54:29.162
of the pretrained encoder. The reward is a

00:54:29.162 --> 00:54:31.372
sparse reward given by an image class So

00:54:31.372 --> 00:54:33.502
it's kind of it's basically the real deal. There's no trickery

00:54:33.502 --> 00:54:35.580
going on here. And optionally, you can

00:54:35.581 --> 00:54:37.632
add in human interventions where if you

00:54:37.632 --> 00:54:39.682
don't like what the robot is doing, that

00:54:39.682 --> 00:54:41.761
when the screen turns red, there's a person that's intervening

00:54:41.762 --> 00:54:44.222
and it's it's almost like a coach giving you a correction

00:54:45.162 --> 00:54:47.182
for how to fix your mistake. So for the PCB insertion,

00:54:47.182 --> 00:54:49.342
there are no human interventions. That's all autonomous. But if

00:54:49.342 --> 00:54:51.606
you have a harder task, like this timing

00:54:51.692 --> 00:54:53.722
belt assembly, you can use human interventions.

00:54:54.122 --> 00:54:56.361
And there are a number of ways to set up this recipe

00:54:56.361 --> 00:54:57.342
so that it works.

00:54:58.462 --> 00:55:00.542
You don't have to use soft DockerKritik, for example,

00:55:00.542 --> 00:55:02.561
which what this uses. There's other methods that'll work.

00:55:02.561 --> 00:55:04.801
The point is to really optimize the whole system

00:55:04.801 --> 00:55:07.152
for very efficiently learning a single narrow task.

00:55:07.331 --> 00:55:09.811
That is not good enough for training a robotic foundation

00:55:09.811 --> 00:55:11.822
model. It is very useful. So I'll

00:55:11.822 --> 00:55:13.902
show you a few videos of this, then explain

00:55:13.902 --> 00:55:15.922
some of the limitations, I'll talk about

00:55:15.922 --> 00:55:18.362
how we can turn this into method

00:55:18.422 --> 00:55:20.237
for training our foundation models.

00:55:21.602 --> 00:55:23.842
So, here are some more videos of a different

00:55:23.842 --> 00:55:25.872
task This is actually a more complex task. It's

00:55:25.872 --> 00:55:27.662
using a bimanual CUKA system.

00:55:27.982 --> 00:55:29.862
To assemble a car dashboard.

00:55:30.902 --> 00:55:32.991
And after trading, this is

00:55:32.992 --> 00:55:35.012
the task. There what makes this difficult, see

00:55:35.252 --> 00:55:37.331
have to align a number of little plastic pins

00:55:37.331 --> 00:55:39.332
so that they line up perfectly with the holes for the

00:55:39.332 --> 00:55:41.322
insertion. That's very hard for a robot to do.

00:55:42.042 --> 00:55:44.122
Here's the timing belt task from before and,

00:55:44.202 --> 00:55:45.842
you know, it's decently robust so somebody

00:55:46.322 --> 00:55:48.401
can go in and mess with the timing belt and the robot will

00:55:48.402 --> 00:55:49.231
recover from that.

00:55:51.032 --> 00:55:53.532
And it's also pretty difficult to do because of the deformable

00:55:53.672 --> 00:55:55.672
objects and stuff like that. And

00:55:55.831 --> 00:55:57.912
And here's a selection of other tasks. So some of them

00:55:57.912 --> 00:55:59.702
are very dynamic like flipping the egg,

00:56:00.122 --> 00:56:02.172
or playing with

00:56:02.172 --> 00:56:04.522
that Jenga tower. Some of them are deformable

00:56:04.582 --> 00:56:06.752
and so on. This is very

00:56:06.752 --> 00:56:08.768
fast, This is all trained directly in the

00:56:08.768 --> 00:56:10.912
real world from images and the success rate gets to a 100%

00:56:10.912 --> 00:56:13.071
on each of these tasks. You also

00:56:13.071 --> 00:56:15.152
get policies that are faster than behavior cloning.

00:56:15.152 --> 00:56:17.252
So here are the blue bars represent

00:56:17.252 --> 00:56:19.412
the cycle time for behavior cloning. The red bars

00:56:19.412 --> 00:56:21.540
represent RL. So it's two

00:56:21.541 --> 00:56:23.361
or more times faster. But

00:56:23.982 --> 00:56:26.222
there's no pretraining going on here, and these

00:56:26.222 --> 00:56:28.291
are specialist policies. So if you

00:56:28.291 --> 00:56:30.452
were to take a poll the policy train for the timing belt

00:56:30.452 --> 00:56:32.542
and make the timing belt like 50% bigger

00:56:32.542 --> 00:56:34.272
or something, then it wouldn't work anymore.

00:56:34.752 --> 00:56:36.832
Okay? So the puzzle that we're faced

00:56:36.832 --> 00:56:38.832
with is, okay, we can

00:56:38.912 --> 00:56:41.232
get very good algorithms for training these specialist

00:56:41.232 --> 00:56:43.352
policies by basically taking out that that red

00:56:43.512 --> 00:56:45.672
circle, removing the foundation model from the equation and

00:56:45.672 --> 00:56:47.701
using very narrow specialized things. Can

00:56:47.702 --> 00:56:49.732
we use them based on the

00:56:49.732 --> 00:56:51.952
bigger? So we investigated

00:56:51.952 --> 00:56:53.981
a this in a method that

00:56:53.982 --> 00:56:56.062
we called RLDG. And the idea

00:56:56.062 --> 00:56:58.142
here is actually very simple. In fact,

00:56:58.142 --> 00:57:00.222
the diagram makes it look more complicated than it

00:57:00.222 --> 00:57:02.341
is. The idea is basically just to use RL

00:57:02.561 --> 00:57:04.602
specialists to generate training data.

00:57:04.922 --> 00:57:07.242
That's it. Just, run RL exactly

00:57:07.242 --> 00:57:09.322
like I showed before, generate training data,

00:57:09.322 --> 00:57:11.732
and just add it into the training set. Your

00:57:11.732 --> 00:57:13.812
favorite general's policy. In this case, we

00:57:13.812 --> 00:57:15.802
used OpenVLA and Octo, but you could use

00:57:15.962 --> 00:57:17.631
anything you want, OpenPy, whatever.

00:57:18.861 --> 00:57:20.942
So as an example of one of these things, we

00:57:20.942 --> 00:57:23.221
trained RL specialist policies

00:57:23.222 --> 00:57:25.302
for inserting different connectors like

00:57:25.302 --> 00:57:26.681
USB and so on,

00:57:27.482 --> 00:57:29.281
use that to produce training data

00:57:29.762 --> 00:57:31.842
fine tuned a specialist model on that, and

00:57:31.842 --> 00:57:33.542
then tested it on other connectors.

00:57:34.122 --> 00:57:36.222
And that's where you get the power of actually,

00:57:36.601 --> 00:57:38.722
integrating these into a generalist model, The

00:57:38.722 --> 00:57:40.962
generalist model can actually generalize to new unseen

00:57:40.962 --> 00:57:43.382
connectors. The individual RL specialists could not.

00:57:43.702 --> 00:57:45.822
So now we're actually seeing some of that

00:57:46.062 --> 00:57:48.142
benefit and this perhaps gives us a hint for how this could

00:57:48.142 --> 00:57:50.242
work in the future. To dig a little bit

00:57:50.242 --> 00:57:51.942
more into the quantitative results,

00:57:52.382 --> 00:57:54.462
the blue bars here represent open VLA, the green

00:57:54.462 --> 00:57:56.742
bars represent Octo, two different foundation models.

00:57:56.902 --> 00:57:59.012
Open VLA is a better one, so it's better across

00:57:59.012 --> 00:58:01.172
the board. And in each pair, the dark

00:58:01.172 --> 00:58:03.262
colored bars are trained on RL data,

00:58:03.422 --> 00:58:05.662
The light colored bars are trained on the

00:58:05.662 --> 00:58:07.462
same amount of successful

00:58:07.762 --> 00:58:10.082
human demonstration data. So this is a controlled experiment.

00:58:10.082 --> 00:58:11.882
RL did use that many demonstrations,

00:58:12.101 --> 00:58:14.261
but we intentionally collected more just so we could have

00:58:14.262 --> 00:58:16.302
an apple to apples comparison with the same

00:58:16.302 --> 00:58:18.322
exact amount of data. And that's kind of

00:58:18.322 --> 00:58:20.742
funny. Like, the RL data actually leads to better generalist

00:58:20.802 --> 00:58:22.816
policies even though it's the same amount and it's the

00:58:22.816 --> 00:58:25.092
same success rate. And you can imagine

00:58:25.152 --> 00:58:27.452
why that might be the case, the RL data comes

00:58:27.512 --> 00:58:29.652
from a real Markovian policy.

00:58:29.652 --> 00:58:31.671
It actually, is something that we know can

00:58:31.672 --> 00:58:33.831
be represented by a neural network, so it provides

00:58:33.832 --> 00:58:35.932
for sort of a better diet of data

00:58:36.212 --> 00:58:37.651
for our generalist policy.

00:58:39.331 --> 00:58:41.302
Now there is something missing from the stress

00:58:41.862 --> 00:58:44.022
that makes it incomplete, that makes it not adequate if we

00:58:44.022 --> 00:58:46.031
want a truly general

00:58:46.091 --> 00:58:48.382
lifelong learning loop. Which is that so far

00:58:48.382 --> 00:58:50.541
the specialist helps the generalist, but the

00:58:50.542 --> 00:58:52.812
generalist does not help specialist.

00:58:53.052 --> 00:58:55.072
So we use these, small

00:58:55.072 --> 00:58:57.151
specialist models to produce training data that goes

00:58:57.152 --> 00:58:59.162
into our robotic foundation model can

00:58:59.162 --> 00:59:01.402
we somehow close the loop? Can we make it so that making

00:59:01.402 --> 00:59:03.482
the generalist better also makes

00:59:03.482 --> 00:59:05.342
the next round of specialist training

00:59:05.912 --> 00:59:08.071
better as well. And if we could do that, then we could

00:59:08.071 --> 00:59:10.342
imagine actually closing the loop on this and taking

00:59:10.342 --> 00:59:12.422
a step towards a truly lifelong system,

00:59:12.422 --> 00:59:14.602
instead of the current one, which is very one directional.

00:59:16.082 --> 00:59:17.831
So we studied this recently,

00:59:18.372 --> 00:59:20.611
in a paper called RLT. And the idea

00:59:20.611 --> 00:59:22.472
we had was well, these specialist,

00:59:22.942 --> 00:59:24.982
policies it's good to keep

00:59:24.982 --> 00:59:27.141
them pretty small because then our l can train them very

00:59:27.142 --> 00:59:29.192
rapidly in the real world. In fact,

00:59:29.192 --> 00:59:31.392
the amount of training just a couple of hours is actually

00:59:31.392 --> 00:59:33.792
less than the amount needed to fine tune the entire

00:59:33.792 --> 00:59:36.142
large foundation model, But training

00:59:36.142 --> 00:59:38.542
them from scratch doesn't let them benefit from the nice

00:59:38.542 --> 00:59:40.601
representations in the foundation model.

00:59:40.601 --> 00:59:42.942
So can we somehow pull out the representations

00:59:43.642 --> 00:59:45.932
from the robotic foundation model?

00:59:46.582 --> 00:59:48.662
Essentially get it to produce like a kind of

00:59:48.821 --> 00:59:50.762
RL token, a token that sort of

00:59:50.902 --> 00:59:53.022
summarizes the knowledge that the foundation model has about

00:59:53.022 --> 00:59:53.922
the current observation,

00:59:55.122 --> 00:59:57.222
and then pass that into this, smaller

00:59:57.282 --> 00:59:59.212
r l actor and critic to get it to be

00:59:59.611 --> 01:00:02.011
a good specialist and then distill it back into the larger

01:00:02.012 --> 01:00:04.152
model. So there are again a few ways to

01:00:04.152 --> 01:00:06.392
set this up, but roughly speaking you need some kind

01:00:06.392 --> 01:00:08.892
of encoder to pull out the representations for

01:00:09.452 --> 01:00:11.551
the robot foundation model and summarize it in

01:00:11.551 --> 01:00:13.472
a in a, concise context.

01:00:14.592 --> 01:00:16.932
You could literally add an a separate

01:00:18.262 --> 01:00:20.522
The way we actually did it is we used an encoder

01:00:20.582 --> 01:00:22.632
decoder architecture You can think of it as a

01:00:22.632 --> 01:00:24.941
very fancy auto encoder that pulls out

01:00:25.182 --> 01:00:27.601
the representations from the VLA and compresses

01:00:27.662 --> 01:00:29.681
them. But again, I think there are multiple ways to do

01:00:29.682 --> 01:00:31.841
it. The point is to get the representations from the VLA

01:00:31.841 --> 01:00:34.112
to be the input to the RL

01:00:34.112 --> 01:00:36.372
specialist instead of forcing it to have its own

01:00:36.672 --> 01:00:37.812
separate observation encoder.

01:00:38.942 --> 01:00:41.182
There's a few additional details to make this work really

01:00:41.182 --> 01:00:43.262
well. The other thing you wanna do is actually take the action

01:00:43.262 --> 01:00:45.502
predicted by the generalist model and provide

01:00:45.502 --> 01:00:47.652
it to the RL learning process

01:00:47.652 --> 01:00:49.981
as well. And that provides

01:00:49.981 --> 01:00:52.341
for a way to constrain exploration. Because

01:00:52.341 --> 01:00:53.882
while you want the RL specialist

01:00:54.692 --> 01:00:56.772
to improve over the base model that it's starting

01:00:56.772 --> 01:00:58.952
from, you don't want it to explore too broadly

01:00:58.962 --> 01:01:01.042
have to essentially, like, rediscover the wheel. So it

01:01:01.042 --> 01:01:03.062
turns out that adding a little regularizer that

01:01:03.222 --> 01:01:05.542
tells it like, hey, all else being equal, try to produce

01:01:05.542 --> 01:01:07.892
an action that's close to the one that the generalist model

01:01:07.892 --> 01:01:10.232
is producing. Actually makes learning faster

01:01:10.922 --> 01:01:13.182
because it, gets it to really first

01:01:13.561 --> 01:01:15.642
internalize whatever strategy the generalist has, and

01:01:15.642 --> 01:01:17.171
then gradually improve it from there.

01:01:19.561 --> 01:01:21.642
And then this efficient, RL actor and

01:01:21.642 --> 01:01:24.011
critic, they receive the representations, reference actions,

01:01:24.412 --> 01:01:26.812
from the robot foundation model and therefore are bootstrapped

01:01:26.812 --> 01:01:29.042
by it. Which lets them learn more complex

01:01:29.262 --> 01:01:31.342
tasks, represent those tasks in a more

01:01:31.342 --> 01:01:33.802
generalizable way, and generally just learn faster.

01:01:34.267 --> 01:01:36.492
So Now, we made one

01:01:36.492 --> 01:01:38.792
more optimization to make this as quick as possible,

01:01:38.792 --> 01:01:41.032
which is that we set it up so that we don't actually

01:01:41.032 --> 01:01:43.100
use RL fine tune the entire

01:01:43.101 --> 01:01:45.221
task. We use RL to fine tune only the segment of

01:01:45.222 --> 01:01:46.852
the task that is most difficult.

01:01:47.412 --> 01:01:49.571
So you'll see a video here of a robot using

01:01:49.572 --> 01:01:51.602
a a screwdriver to assemble

01:01:51.602 --> 01:01:53.922
a screw. Most of the task is actually

01:01:53.922 --> 01:01:55.702
just executed by the base journalist

01:01:56.002 --> 01:01:58.252
model. And at one point, we switched to

01:01:58.252 --> 01:02:00.332
the RL specialist for just the difficult part of

01:02:00.332 --> 01:02:02.342
the task, it needs to practice. So this

01:02:02.342 --> 01:02:04.662
is all with the base model, and then, the text

01:02:04.662 --> 01:02:06.751
at the bottom will switch. When the RL

01:02:06.752 --> 01:02:08.772
is active, and that's just for the actual screwdriver

01:02:08.972 --> 01:02:11.052
assembly part because that's the part that requires

01:02:11.052 --> 01:02:13.232
very precise alignment, and

01:02:13.492 --> 01:02:15.552
usually takes, the most extra time. The

01:02:15.552 --> 01:02:17.771
robot makes a mistake. And

01:02:17.771 --> 01:02:19.851
here are some more tasks that were learned with this method,

01:02:19.851 --> 01:02:22.152
assembling the screw, fastening

01:02:22.152 --> 01:02:24.372
a zip tie, inserting, Ethernet cable.

01:02:24.372 --> 01:02:26.312
So you'll notice that these tasks all have something

01:02:26.452 --> 01:02:28.502
in common, which is they all have some

01:02:29.142 --> 01:02:31.162
fairly, coarse phases where you just have to

01:02:31.162 --> 01:02:33.322
manipulate the object in a reasonable way. And then they

01:02:33.322 --> 01:02:34.691
have some very precise phases

01:02:35.331 --> 01:02:37.412
where you have to do it very, very carefully. And it's

01:02:37.412 --> 01:02:39.472
those precise phases that are being

01:02:39.472 --> 01:02:41.692
most improved the RL method.

01:02:42.652 --> 01:02:44.672
So there you can see the the zip tie assembly

01:02:44.732 --> 01:02:45.132
happening.

01:02:49.982 --> 01:02:52.222
The zip tie is very hard because it's actually holding both

01:02:52.222 --> 01:02:54.362
ends of the tie in the air, and they're all, like, wiggling all

01:02:54.362 --> 01:02:56.442
over the place. And in some sense, the

01:02:56.442 --> 01:02:58.572
robot actually makes it look easy once it's learned the task with

01:02:58.572 --> 01:03:00.482
our role properly. So

01:03:01.362 --> 01:03:03.582
Quantitatively, this actually works really well. So

01:03:04.622 --> 01:03:07.042
the bar graph here shows the amount

01:03:07.342 --> 01:03:09.492
of the the, throughput,

01:03:09.492 --> 01:03:11.512
which is the number of successful

01:03:11.572 --> 01:03:13.802
executions of the task per unit time.

01:03:13.802 --> 01:03:16.202
This is a good way to summarize both success rate

01:03:16.202 --> 01:03:18.282
and speed. And you can see that the throughput

01:03:18.342 --> 01:03:20.562
on each of these tasks improves a lot. So the

01:03:20.882 --> 01:03:22.980
bar graphs show separately the just

01:03:22.981 --> 01:03:25.081
the stages fine tuned with RL which are the

01:03:25.082 --> 01:03:27.321
four bars on the right and the full task including

01:03:27.322 --> 01:03:29.341
the parts that are not fine tuned on the left. But, of

01:03:29.341 --> 01:03:31.502
course, the difficult phase is usually the bottleneck

01:03:31.502 --> 01:03:33.282
for these tasks. So, the

01:03:33.732 --> 01:03:36.052
improving the difficult phase improves the whole task

01:03:36.052 --> 01:03:38.132
a lot. The speed of these things is

01:03:38.132 --> 01:03:40.312
actually quite good. So this is a

01:03:40.312 --> 01:03:42.552
histogram of episode length. The

01:03:42.552 --> 01:03:44.932
red section is the base

01:03:44.932 --> 01:03:47.261
generalist policy. The green section is

