review. So as you probably

02:22:18.032 --> 02:22:20.271
heard, there's this tool called Pangram that can

02:22:20.271 --> 02:22:22.282
detect AI generated text with

02:22:22.282 --> 02:22:24.072
a very low false positive rate.

02:22:24.352 --> 02:22:27.072
And 21%

02:22:27.072 --> 02:22:28.452
of iClearer peer reviews

02:22:29.252 --> 02:22:31.553
were LLM generated. And so even

02:22:31.553 --> 02:22:33.632
if you abstain from using an LLM and writing

02:22:33.632 --> 02:22:35.362
your paper, you just, like,

02:22:36.322 --> 02:22:38.292
the human grit, you wrote the whole thing yourself.

02:22:38.452 --> 02:22:40.311
You're still gonna be subject to LLM

02:22:40.692 --> 02:22:42.792
generated reviews in reviewing your paper

02:22:43.372 --> 02:22:45.152
and whether it's worthy of scientific publication.

02:22:45.692 --> 02:22:47.392
And what's messed up about that

02:22:47.732 --> 02:22:49.682
is that, we looked at

02:22:50.053 --> 02:22:52.152
the difference in the strengths

02:22:52.452 --> 02:22:54.812
and weaknesses that both human reviewers

02:22:54.812 --> 02:22:57.312
and LLM reviewers focus on for iCLEAR.

02:22:57.652 --> 02:22:59.912
And we found that LLM generated reviews

02:23:00.432 --> 02:23:02.593
focus on significantly different

02:23:02.593 --> 02:23:04.762
criteria whether this is a good paper.

02:23:05.022 --> 02:23:07.503
So what you're seeing in these plots is human

02:23:07.503 --> 02:23:09.692
reviews would be this dash line down the middle.

02:23:09.852 --> 02:23:11.932
That would be 0% change from the human

02:23:11.932 --> 02:23:13.952
baseline would be the LM generator reviews

02:23:13.952 --> 02:23:15.951
focused on the same criteria as the humans.

02:23:16.352 --> 02:23:18.772
But what we see is that when LLMs are writing reviews,

02:23:19.571 --> 02:23:21.111
focus significantly less on clarity,

02:23:22.082 --> 02:23:24.502
writing, and presentation, and significantly more

02:23:24.642 --> 02:23:26.772
on practical application, reproducibility,

02:23:27.312 --> 02:23:29.762
and scalability. So

02:23:29.962 --> 02:23:32.221
the fact that we're adopting these

02:23:32.442 --> 02:23:34.061
LLMs en masse is actually changing

02:23:34.522 --> 02:23:36.312
how we make decisions

02:23:36.803 --> 02:23:38.822
in terms of how we run our scientific

02:23:38.883 --> 02:23:40.262
institutions like iCLEAR.

02:23:41.292 --> 02:23:43.532
This is just happening so rapidly, and it's poorly

02:23:43.532 --> 02:23:45.751
understood. So you might say,

02:23:45.752 --> 02:23:47.802
look. You know, even though

02:23:47.802 --> 02:23:50.022
these reviewers that presumably have their

02:23:50.022 --> 02:23:52.182
professional reputation on the line when they're having

02:23:52.182 --> 02:23:54.082
an LLM do their job for them,

02:23:55.122 --> 02:23:57.332
you know, maybe they're just lazy.

02:23:57.332 --> 02:23:59.492
Maybe they're just, like, bad at using the LLM. They just,

02:23:59.492 --> 02:24:01.692
like, flubbed it off to the LM. Like,

02:24:01.963 --> 02:24:04.043
this is literally coming from a coauthor on this

02:24:04.043 --> 02:24:06.172
paper. Right? Complaint would be, well,

02:24:06.173 --> 02:24:08.572
I'm so good at prompting. I'm so good at using

02:24:08.572 --> 02:24:10.721
LMS. Like, this isn't gonna happen to me. It's

02:24:10.721 --> 02:24:12.522
gonna still write in the way that I want.

02:24:13.242 --> 02:24:15.312
So to address this criticism, what

02:24:15.312 --> 02:24:17.652
we did was we ran a human user

02:24:17.793 --> 02:24:18.932
study where we have

02:24:19.811 --> 02:24:22.311
people interact repeatedly with an LLM

02:24:22.531 --> 02:24:24.662
over time to continue giving it feedback and

02:24:24.662 --> 02:24:26.742
prompting it and correcting it. And to see

02:24:26.742 --> 02:24:28.282
if it could avoid this effect.

02:24:29.552 --> 02:24:31.821
And what we see is that

02:24:32.442 --> 02:24:34.521
for some people so we had a control group. We

02:24:34.521 --> 02:24:36.842
had a control group people that had no access

02:24:37.141 --> 02:24:39.382
to the LM. They had to answer a question

02:24:39.382 --> 02:24:41.502
called, you know, does money buy happiness?

02:24:41.502 --> 02:24:43.561
They had to write an essay about that. So we see

02:24:43.561 --> 02:24:45.572
that in the control group, this human group

02:24:45.892 --> 02:24:48.132
40% were neutral, and there was about

02:24:48.132 --> 02:24:48.982
a slight

02:24:53.602 --> 02:24:55.622
In the experimental group, some

02:24:55.622 --> 02:24:57.782
people actually abstained from using LLMs at

02:24:57.782 --> 02:24:59.812
all. Or maybe they just asked the LLM for,

02:24:59.812 --> 02:25:01.972
like, a little bit of feedback, like, more like

02:25:01.972 --> 02:25:04.101
a search tool, like a Google search way

02:25:04.101 --> 02:25:06.272
of using the LM. That's this LM influence group.

02:25:06.432 --> 02:25:08.272
They look pretty similar to the control group.

02:25:08.753 --> 02:25:11.253
But there was this group of users that

02:25:11.392 --> 02:25:13.242
basically asked the LM to write

02:25:13.482 --> 02:25:15.722
big chunks of the essay. They'd say, write me an intro

02:25:15.722 --> 02:25:18.181
saying this. Okay. Now add a second paragraph

02:25:18.181 --> 02:25:20.422
saying this. And they were directing the LLM about

02:25:20.662 --> 02:25:22.902
what it should write, but they were largely

02:25:22.902 --> 02:25:24.882
relying on the LLM to generate the text.

02:25:25.282 --> 02:25:27.702
And what we see is that for those users,

02:25:28.292 --> 02:25:30.471
the essays they ended up producing are significantly

02:25:30.611 --> 02:25:32.632
different than the human written essays.

02:25:33.012 --> 02:25:35.252
So we see a 70% increase

02:25:35.252 --> 02:25:37.401
in the amount of people just adopt no

02:25:37.401 --> 02:25:39.481
position at all, just having a neutral tone in their

02:25:39.481 --> 02:25:41.602
essay. So just it's kind of blandification

02:25:41.742 --> 02:25:43.842
of not really saying anything. And we

02:25:43.842 --> 02:25:46.081
actually see the proportion of people saying money

02:25:46.082 --> 02:25:48.252
buys happiness changes pretty

02:25:48.252 --> 02:25:50.592
significantly when you rely on the LMM.

02:25:50.812 --> 02:25:53.133
So again, we're seeing that LLMs are changing

02:25:53.133 --> 02:25:55.262
the actual meaning of what we write when

02:25:55.262 --> 02:25:56.182
we rely on them.

02:25:57.633 --> 02:25:59.772
You might ask, in what ways do they

02:25:59.772 --> 02:26:02.122
change meaning? I found this

02:26:02.422 --> 02:26:04.812
really interesting. The LLM not only makes the writing

02:26:04.812 --> 02:26:06.612
more emotional, talking about

02:26:06.852 --> 02:26:08.932
positive, negative, and trust emotions,

02:26:08.932 --> 02:26:10.952
but it also makes it more logical

02:26:11.102 --> 02:26:13.141
and statistical. So

02:26:13.141 --> 02:26:15.542
what's the deal with that? Why would it be both more emotional

02:26:15.542 --> 02:26:16.842
and sort of more argumentative?

02:26:17.883 --> 02:26:20.043
My hypothesis is that this is because

02:26:20.043 --> 02:26:22.303
of the large scale effect of RLHF.

02:26:23.101 --> 02:26:25.181
So basically, elements are not good

02:26:25.181 --> 02:26:27.312
at adjust adjusting and adapting

02:26:27.532 --> 02:26:29.713
online to each user's individual intentions,

02:26:29.852 --> 02:26:31.912
needs, context, you know,

02:26:31.912 --> 02:26:33.992
piece of writing that they're doing. But

02:26:33.992 --> 02:26:36.072
they have to optimize for human feedback over

02:26:36.072 --> 02:26:38.293
billions of people. So what does that get you?

02:26:38.293 --> 02:26:40.372
I think that gets you a policy that's sort of overly

02:26:40.372 --> 02:26:42.641
manipulative and persuasive. It's like

02:26:42.641 --> 02:26:44.802
talking about emotions. It's talking about arguments. It's

02:26:44.802 --> 02:26:47.072
looking for that thumbs up. Even though it's not actually

02:26:47.072 --> 02:26:48.712
that good at customizing writing.

02:26:50.391 --> 02:26:52.572
So this seems scary, but I would

02:26:52.572 --> 02:26:54.811
just say, like, I don't think LLM adoption is likely

02:26:54.811 --> 02:26:56.882
to slow. I mean, if you use Claude Code, are you going

02:26:56.882 --> 02:26:58.712
back? Right?

02:26:59.133 --> 02:27:01.373
What we see in our study is that compared

02:27:01.373 --> 02:27:03.461
to the control group, the people who've

02:27:03.462 --> 02:27:05.332
heavily relied on the LLM

02:27:05.492 --> 02:27:07.891
said that the writing was less in their voice

02:27:07.891 --> 02:27:10.212
and less creative. But actually,

02:27:10.212 --> 02:27:12.003
they were equally satisfied

02:27:12.303 --> 02:27:14.332
with the results. Right? It's a

02:27:14.332 --> 02:27:16.652
lot easier to write an essay with an LM

02:27:16.652 --> 02:27:18.832
than write it yourself. So I don't think this

02:27:18.832 --> 02:27:20.622
is going away anytime soon.

02:27:20.892 --> 02:27:23.372
Actually there was this really interesting Nature paper

02:27:23.372 --> 02:27:25.712
that showed for scientists, if you

02:27:25.852 --> 02:27:28.021
rely on AI in writing your papers, you can

02:27:28.021 --> 02:27:30.181
increase your output by something like

02:27:30.181 --> 02:27:32.182
five x You can increase your

02:27:32.182 --> 02:27:34.102
citation count by three x.

02:27:34.503 --> 02:27:36.602
But for science as a whole, for everyone,

02:27:38.042 --> 02:27:40.382
our global creativity in the scientific

02:27:40.441 --> 02:27:42.452
enterprise is decreased. Like,

02:27:42.452 --> 02:27:44.873
the topics we focus on are just less diverse.

02:27:45.602 --> 02:27:47.682
So it's now a social dilemma. Right? It's you have

02:27:47.682 --> 02:27:50.102
an individual incentive to rely on LLMs

02:27:50.542 --> 02:27:52.702
But if we all do that, science is

02:27:52.702 --> 02:27:54.932
gonna be worse. So

02:27:54.932 --> 02:27:56.553
it it's pretty interesting. Right?

02:27:56.982 --> 02:27:59.062
So you might say, how can we fix this? Like,

02:27:59.062 --> 02:28:01.192
how is this about, you know, lifelong learning

02:28:01.192 --> 02:28:03.312
and adapting online? I

02:28:03.312 --> 02:28:05.092
think the thing we need to be doing

02:28:05.742 --> 02:28:08.142
is minimizing this counterfactual metric.

02:28:08.142 --> 02:28:10.192
So if you remember, this data set

02:28:10.192 --> 02:28:12.612
gave us this counterfactual of we had

02:28:12.932 --> 02:28:15.002
what the human would have written had

02:28:15.002 --> 02:28:17.083
there been no LLM involved. And then we

02:28:17.083 --> 02:28:19.102
looked at the difference in what the LLM

02:28:19.162 --> 02:28:21.382
produced. And so I think the real metric

02:28:21.382 --> 02:28:23.052
which we should be striving for

02:28:23.607 --> 02:28:25.842
LMs is to accelerate people

02:28:25.842 --> 02:28:27.772
in doing what they would have done

02:28:27.852 --> 02:28:30.253
if they'd had more time, effort, information,

02:28:30.253 --> 02:28:32.262
whatever. Not doing

02:28:32.262 --> 02:28:34.442
something else entirely. So Claude

02:28:34.503 --> 02:28:36.662
Code should architect the code base in the way

02:28:36.662 --> 02:28:38.682
you would have done it. So it's, like, maintainable.

02:28:39.222 --> 02:28:41.231
Right? And not

02:28:41.231 --> 02:28:43.311
just, like, do its own thing that's something

02:28:43.311 --> 02:28:45.641
completely different than what you would have written and therefore

02:28:45.641 --> 02:28:47.262
is you know,

02:28:48.232 --> 02:28:50.392
uninterpretable and unvarnatable. So we're

02:28:50.392 --> 02:28:52.691
trying to minimize this metric. So

02:28:52.691 --> 02:28:53.752
I actually think

02:28:54.712 --> 02:28:56.492
a big way to do this is personalization.

02:28:57.392 --> 02:28:59.173
So I think when people hear personalization,

02:28:59.472 --> 02:29:01.713
sometimes it's a bit controversial. They have a reaction

02:29:01.713 --> 02:29:03.832
like, I oh, well, with, you know, filter

02:29:03.833 --> 02:29:06.282
bubbles, right, like personalization in social media.

02:29:06.282 --> 02:29:07.742
But I think in terms of LLMs,

02:29:08.372 --> 02:29:10.452
personalization just makes them better and more

02:29:10.452 --> 02:29:12.593
useful. So it's actually doing

02:29:12.593 --> 02:29:14.652
what you want the LLM to do in

02:29:14.652 --> 02:29:16.562
the way that you uniquely would do it

02:29:17.122 --> 02:29:19.471
and therefore preserving your capabilities,

02:29:19.532 --> 02:29:21.432
but just accelerating you. So

02:29:21.593 --> 02:29:23.452
we've been doing a bunch of work on personalization.

02:29:24.672 --> 02:29:26.832
Kind of, in the context of reward

02:29:26.832 --> 02:29:29.012
model learning. Like, how do you determine

02:29:29.553 --> 02:29:31.633
like, if you if you're familiar the BTL model

02:29:31.633 --> 02:29:34.092
used in RHF, actually this unimodal

02:29:34.152 --> 02:29:36.412
preference model. So it assumes that

02:29:36.412 --> 02:29:38.492
everyone has the exact same preference function, which is

02:29:38.492 --> 02:29:40.572
just straight wrong. So we've been working on ways

02:29:40.572 --> 02:29:42.432
to sort of customize that and learn

02:29:42.673 --> 02:29:45.042
individualized preference functions for different users.

02:29:45.601 --> 02:29:47.762
But, the work I want to talk about today

02:29:47.762 --> 02:29:49.852
is basically saying, instead of

02:29:49.852 --> 02:29:51.932
conditioning on this long history

02:29:51.932 --> 02:29:54.042
of the user, could

02:29:54.181 --> 02:29:56.212
we actually learn online

02:29:57.172 --> 02:29:59.242
during the conversation to adapt to the user.

02:29:59.302 --> 02:30:01.542
So we personalize to the user by just talking

02:30:01.542 --> 02:30:03.592
to them. And so this is this work

02:30:03.592 --> 02:30:05.852
that, we just presented, in NERVs.

02:30:06.332 --> 02:30:08.412
It also won this Madrona Prize for

02:30:08.412 --> 02:30:10.562
its commercialization potential. Which

02:30:10.562 --> 02:30:12.882
was a little bit hilarious because we snuck

02:30:12.882 --> 02:30:15.282
it out, just underneath the Google

02:30:15.282 --> 02:30:16.502
publication van.

02:30:17.312 --> 02:30:19.423
Almost got banned, and and it it

02:30:19.423 --> 02:30:21.582
got out. So I'm really excited about this.

02:30:21.662 --> 02:30:23.242
First author is Yan Ming Wan.

02:30:24.503 --> 02:30:26.583
But the idea here is you wanna

02:30:26.583 --> 02:30:28.622
be able to personalize to the user

02:30:29.181 --> 02:30:31.422
online during the conversation. So it's

02:30:31.422 --> 02:30:33.232
kind of a meta learning problem actually.

02:30:33.872 --> 02:30:35.732
How do you, during the conversation,

02:30:36.542 --> 02:30:38.782
obtain information about the user and adjust

02:30:38.782 --> 02:30:40.552
your policy online? And how do you

02:30:40.872 --> 02:30:42.771
train a model that can do that? Right?

02:30:43.431 --> 02:30:45.891
So what we're doing is we're setting up a training

02:30:46.192 --> 02:30:48.272
paradigm where each episode, you've

02:30:48.272 --> 02:30:49.532
got a new user

02:30:50.412 --> 02:30:51.632
and you're tasked with

02:30:52.802 --> 02:30:55.041
like, adjusting to that user's particular needs

02:30:55.042 --> 02:30:57.372
in order to accomplish a sparse

02:30:57.803 --> 02:31:00.122
multi turn task. So the key aspect

02:31:00.122 --> 02:31:02.152
of this is the is the multi turn task.

02:31:02.463 --> 02:31:04.703
To it. It's like long horizon reasoning

02:31:04.703 --> 02:31:06.552
about how to adjust to the user

02:31:06.792 --> 02:31:09.032
during the conversation. The way

02:31:09.032 --> 02:31:11.502
it's implemented is we have

02:31:11.641 --> 02:31:13.962
a user model that's predicting something

02:31:13.962 --> 02:31:16.213
about the user. So here we have, like, the user's

02:31:16.432 --> 02:31:18.682
particular learning style in an education task.

02:31:19.642 --> 02:31:21.722
And the you the mod we're giving it an

02:31:21.722 --> 02:31:23.842
intrinsic reward so that it gets a reward

02:31:23.842 --> 02:31:25.942
if after it says

02:31:25.942 --> 02:31:27.642
something and the user responds,

02:31:27.962 --> 02:31:30.122
it's able to learn more about the

02:31:30.122 --> 02:31:32.391
user and increase the accuracy of

02:31:32.391 --> 02:31:34.422
its, reward model. And the idea

02:31:34.422 --> 02:31:36.522
is that giving that intrinsic curiosity

02:31:37.382 --> 02:31:39.242
motivation is going to help it,

02:31:39.562 --> 02:31:41.803
learn this eventually sparse reward

02:31:41.803 --> 02:31:42.303
task.

02:31:43.873 --> 02:31:45.972
So this was project was a bit

02:31:45.972 --> 02:31:47.652
of a nightmare of just, like,

02:31:48.133 --> 02:31:49.912
many, many, many LLMs interacting

02:31:50.972 --> 02:31:53.123
And in particular, one,

02:31:53.123 --> 02:31:55.362
I think, weakness of all a bunch of work in

02:31:55.362 --> 02:31:57.412
this line that people are now starting work on

02:31:57.412 --> 02:31:59.593
including myself is we have to simulate

02:32:00.402 --> 02:32:02.408
pretend human user. To

02:32:02.408 --> 02:32:04.322
get the scale that we need. So we have an LLM

02:32:04.752 --> 02:32:07.062
simulating the human user. It's we're

02:32:07.062 --> 02:32:09.402
calling it the environment model, and it's frozen.

02:32:09.852 --> 02:32:12.093
It's being used to simulate a conversation with

02:32:12.093 --> 02:32:14.352
the policy model, which we're trying to train.

02:32:14.771 --> 02:32:16.861
The reward model is giving this end of

02:32:16.861 --> 02:32:19.181
conversation, sparse reward. Like, did you teach the student

02:32:19.181 --> 02:32:20.682
effectively? Did they learn something?

02:32:21.803 --> 02:32:23.822
The user model, somewhat confusingly named,

02:32:24.673 --> 02:32:26.833
is our thing that we're integrating in particular

02:32:26.833 --> 02:32:28.992
in this work which is trying to predict

02:32:28.992 --> 02:32:31.071
characteristics of the user that we're trying to learn about. So you can

02:32:31.071 --> 02:32:33.022
think of you're familiar with, like, classic

02:32:33.162 --> 02:32:35.022
curiosity, motivation in RL.

02:32:35.322 --> 02:32:37.362
You might have a world model. Here, what is

02:32:37.362 --> 02:32:39.682
the world in, for a dialogue system

02:32:39.682 --> 02:32:41.923
for an LM? It's the user. It's talking

02:32:41.923 --> 02:32:44.162
to the user. Right? So we're modeling

02:32:44.162 --> 02:32:45.852
the user. We get this

02:32:46.391 --> 02:32:48.753
intrinsic reward if we improve the accuracy

02:32:48.812 --> 02:32:50.673
of our belief about the user.

02:32:51.452 --> 02:32:53.532
And so Yanming formulated all of

02:32:53.532 --> 02:32:55.601
these in terms of potential based reward

02:32:55.601 --> 02:32:57.592
shaping because you might notice that

02:32:57.832 --> 02:32:59.912
this is a POMDP. There's something hidden

02:32:59.912 --> 02:33:02.271
about the user that we don't know. And

02:33:02.271 --> 02:33:04.452
potential based reward shaping says, if I

02:33:04.752 --> 02:33:06.762
formulate the, reward looking like

02:33:06.762 --> 02:33:08.872
this, then it's not going to

02:33:08.872 --> 02:33:10.952
change the optimal policy. So in other words,

02:33:10.952 --> 02:33:13.191
I can add this curiosity incentive for learning about

02:33:13.191 --> 02:33:15.382
the user, but I won't change the eventual

02:33:15.843 --> 02:33:17.682
optimal behavior for interacting with that user.

02:33:18.642 --> 02:33:20.682
And so I will talk about this more at

02:33:20.682 --> 02:33:22.752
the end, but you'll notice that we

02:33:22.992 --> 02:33:25.232
formulated this as you might expect. Like, a classic

02:33:25.232 --> 02:33:27.612
curiosity objective might be information

02:33:27.992 --> 02:33:30.161
gain. Right? Did I gain some did I reduce

02:33:30.162 --> 02:33:32.322
my uncertainty in my model of the

02:33:32.322 --> 02:33:34.333
user as a result of my

02:33:34.333 --> 02:33:36.742
conversation? But the problem with that is

02:33:36.902 --> 02:33:38.982
the model uncertainty, the uncertainty of our

02:33:38.982 --> 02:33:41.072
user model is not well calibrated.

02:33:41.072 --> 02:33:43.392
